398	        <div class="faq-item">
399	          <h3>Where is my data stored? Does anything leave my machine?</h3>
400	          <p>The cache lives in the repository it describes, and it stays on your machine. Notes are under <code>.thinker/local/</code>, which git ignores; there is no team sharing or sync. Notes that an older version committed under <code>.thinker/notes/</code> are still read. <code>.thinker/config.json</code> holds the configuration. Per machine, <code>~/.thinker/</code> holds the installed app, the usage log every serving is recorded in, and the ranking model. The Context report is read from the transcripts your agents already write; nothing is copied out of them.</p>
401	          <p>Three things leave the machine, and nothing else:</p>
402	          <ul>
403	            <li><b>Model calls go to the provider you already use for your agent.</b> Learning and verification run through your agent's own login (<code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>gemini</code>, <code>cursor</code>, or an Anthropic API key), or, in a desktop app (Claude Desktop, the Codex app, Cursor), through the app's own session. A session is distilled from a condensed transcript; verification sends a note with the diff of the code it rests on. Thinker runs no model and stores nothing of its own in the cloud.</li>
404	            <li><b>Telemetry is counts only.</b> An hourly report carries numbers: notes served, verdicts, token totals, the sums of the holdout comparison, with an install id and a hashed device id. It contains no code, note bodies, prompt text, file paths or repository URLs. <code>THINKER_TELEMETRY=off</code> or <code>{ "telemetry": false }</code> in the config stops it.</li>
405	            <li><b>The ranking model is downloaded once at install</b> (about 23 MB) and runs on your machine. Ranking never calls a service.</li>
406	          </ul>
407	          <p>This page listens on 127.0.0.1 only.</p>
408	          <p><b>A repository with sensitive content can be turned off</b> with <code>thinker disable</code> inside it. Thinker then does nothing there, in any clone or worktree: the hooks and the MCP server serve nothing, no session is distilled, and nothing is written into it. <code>thinker enable</code> turns it back on.</p>
409	        </div>
410	
411	        <div class="faq-item">
412	          <h3>How does the cache work? What does it do to make my agents faster?</h3>
413	          <p>A note is a short rule, map or how-to about one part of the code, with <code>file:Symbol</code> pointers and a hash of the code it rests on. The cache is a set of such notes, and the work is in putting the right one in front of the agent without being asked:</p>
414	          <ul>
415	            <li><b>On each request</b> a hook picks the lexical candidates, then a local ranking model reads the request against them and serves up to two notes, only those it scores relevant. The agent starts at the code instead of searching for it.</li>
416	            <li><b>On each file edit</b> the rules resting on that file are served, so a trap on that code is in view while it is changed.</li>
417	            <li><b>On demand</b>, the MCP tool <code>lookup</code> answers one open question or fetches a note the hook listed; skill cards are among the notes.</li>
418	            <li><b>Changes queue a recheck.</b> Serving checks candidate notes against your working tree. Ordinary code edits leave notes usable pending verification. Missing anchors and reported contradictions are held back until resolved.</li>
419	            <li><b>Once per session.</b> A note is served once, not for one-word turns, and not at all when nothing fits.</li>
420	          </ul>
421	          <p>The cache keeps itself: each session is distilled once when it ends, pending and stale notes used or requested recently are re-verified every three days, notes the sessions never acted on are archived, and a note nobody was served in two weeks is deleted.</p>
422	        </div>
423	
424	        <div class="faq-item">
425	          <h3>Where do the notes come from? Can I build the cache up front?</h3>
426	          <p>From your own sessions, and from nothing else. When a session ends, a model reads a condensed transcript of it (what was asked, read, run and changed) and saves what the session worked out that the next one would otherwise work out again, usually nothing or one note. The same call grades the notes the session was served: confirmed, contradicted (the note is corrected) or unused. A quiet session, with nothing served, no edit, no failure and little exploring, is skipped.</p>
427	          <ul>
428	            <li><b>There is no build step.</b> <code>thinker setup</code> connects your agents and nothing else; every git repository starts with an empty cache, and the hooks serve from it once it holds a few notes.</li>
429	            <li><b>The spend is bounded.</b> One model call per session, through your agent's login, and learning and maintenance together stop at a daily token cap (<code>maintain.dailyTokens</code> in <code>.thinker/config.json</code>).</li>
430	            <li><b>You can add your own.</b> <code>thinker add</code> takes a note you wrote, and telling your agent to remember something makes it a note when the session is distilled.</li>
431	            <li><b>You can stop it.</b> <code>{ "learn": { "sessions": false } }</code> in the config stops learning from sessions in a repository; <code>THINKER_NO_LEARN=1</code> stops all learning.</li>
432	          </ul>
433	        </div>
434	
435	        <div class="faq-item">
436	          <h3>Can Thinker cut what my agents load into every session?</h3>
