453	// Three answers to the three reasons a request the cache was asked gets nothing (EMPTY_REASONS below).
454	// Measured on production telemetry over the ten days to 2026-10-28: of 3,561 requests the cache was
455	// actually searched for, 70 were answered, and the misses were `no-match` 1,983 (56%),
456	// `ranker-rejected` 949 (27%) and `only-stale` 556 (16%). Each lever below addresses one of them.
457	//
458	// All three are off by default and stay off until an Artemis experiment measures them in agent
459	// sessions against the shipped strategy on the same tasks: they change the serving pool, the
460	// cross-encoder's veto and the freshness rule, which is exactly what a cache-and-serving change may
461	// not do by default without evidence (AGENTS.md). Offline replays are not evidence. A switch here
462	// going on by default is its own pull request citing its experiment.
463	//
464	// `serving` in .thinker/config.json, or the environment, which wins over the config:
465	//   denseFallback  THINKER_SERVE_DENSE_FALLBACK=on|off  a request no note shares a word with is
466	//                  scored by the local embedding model instead (dense.js:denseScores, MiniLM), and
467	//                  the best note above `denseFloor` is served. Only when the lexical ranking found
468	//                  nothing at all, so it never reorders a ranking that worked. Note that a dense
469	//                  bi-encoder blended into the ranking measured no gain (memory, 2026-10); this is
470	//                  the untested different case of no lexical candidate whatsoever.
471	//                  `denseFloor` is 0.35, the relevance `pack` already requires of any note it packs
472	//                  (rank.js:pack `minRel`), so a densely-found note clears the same bar as every
473	//                  other served note rather than a bar invented for it. The cosines of this blend run
474	//                  low: a note that answers a request in none of its words scored 0.37 in a check on
475	//                  2026-10-28, so a stricter floor would have kept the lever from ever firing. The
476	//                  right floor is one of the things the experiment has to settle.
477	//   ceRescue       THINKER_SERVE_CE_RESCUE=on|off       when the cross-encoder keeps none of the
478	//                  candidates, the best one it turned down is served anyway rather than nothing. The
479	//                  cross-encoder's choice being final is what makes it reject everything on 27% of
480	//                  attempted requests; this trades some of that precision for coverage.
481	//   serveStale     THINKER_SERVE_STALE=on|off           the hooks serve a stale note, with the
482	//                  ⚠ STALE banner it already carries, instead of holding it back. This repository
483	//                  declares "the prompt hooks serve no stale note" as a mutable desired behavior, so
484	//                  turning it on here revises that behavior and must say so.
485	export const SERVING_DEFAULTS = { denseFallback: false, ceRescue: false, serveStale: false, denseFloor: 0.35 };
486	export function servingConfig(store) {
487	  const c = store?.config?.().serving;
488	  const cfg = { ...SERVING_DEFAULTS, ...(c && typeof c === 'object' ? c : {}) };
489	  const e = process.env;
490	  const flag = (name, key) => { if (e[name] === 'on') cfg[key] = true; else if (e[name] === 'off') cfg[key] = false; };
491	  flag('THINKER_SERVE_DENSE_FALLBACK', 'denseFallback');
492	  flag('THINKER_SERVE_CE_RESCUE', 'ceRescue');
493	  flag('THINKER_SERVE_STALE', 'serveStale');
494	  if (e.THINKER_SERVE_DENSE_FLOOR !== undefined && e.THINKER_SERVE_DENSE_FLOOR !== '') cfg.denseFloor = Number(e.THINKER_SERVE_DENSE_FLOOR);
495	  if (!Number.isFinite(cfg.denseFloor)) cfg.denseFloor = SERVING_DEFAULTS.denseFloor;
496	  return cfg;
497	}
